course: dictionary
lesson: 2740
title: Disability: respect and differences
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A disability is a condition that makes some things harder for a person to do, such as seeing, hearing, walking, learning or communicating. «People with disabilities want to be talked to and treated like everyone else.» Disabilities are not a reason to treat someone differently or unkindly.
explain: Some disabilities are visible, like using a wheelchair. Others are invisible, like diabetes, a heart problem, autism or dyslexia. «Many disabilities are invisible, so believe someone who says they need a snack or a rest.» You cannot always tell by looking at someone.
explain: Kindness means including everyone, asking before you help, talking to the person directly, and not staring or teasing. Keep ramps, lifts and disabled parking spaces free, because «disabled access» helps people get around.
explain: People with disabilities do many things, sometimes in a different way. They may use wheelchairs, hearing aids, guide dogs, sign language or Braille. «Mean words about disability hurt, so do not use them and say they are not okay.»
fact: A disability is a condition that makes some things harder for a person to do.
fact: «People with disabilities» means people who have a disability.
fact: «Disabled access» means ramps, lifts and routes that people with disabilities can use.
fact: Some disabilities are invisible, such as autism, dyslexia or diabetes.
fact: The Paralympics celebrate athletes with disabilities.
exercise: What is a disability? | A word for being very tired after school | A type of hearing aid | A kind of fast sport for runners | *A condition that makes some things harder for a person to do
exercise: Which disability can you NOT see? | *Diabetes | Needing a guide dog | Using sign language | Using a wheelchair
exercise: «Mean words about disability hurt, so do not use them and say they are not okay.» What is the kind thing to do? | *Ask before you help someone | Stare at a classmate's hearing aid | Block the ramp with bikes | Tease a classmate who walks slowly
test: «Many disabilities are invisible, so believe someone who says they need a snack or a rest.» Why should you believe them? | *Because you cannot always see a disability | Because rest is never important | Because they must be lying if they look well | Because snacks are always needed at school
test: A classmate uses a wheelchair and you want to help. What should you do? | *Ask the person first, then talk to them directly | Stare at the wheelchair until they leave | Talk only to their helper and ignore them | Pick up the wheelchair and push it without asking
exam: What do the Paralympics celebrate? | Cars that drive on ice | Teachers who work in schools | Only the fastest runners in the world | *Athletes with disabilities
